  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe 12Z objectively analyzed UA charts observed that the subtropical \nridge, which is currently centered over the MS River Valley, has \nstrengthened slightly compared to 24 hours ago, with geopotential \nheights now at 592 dam at 500 mb. The Omega Block is beginning to \ncollapse due to a well-defined, positively-tilted shortwave trough \nthat is ejecting over the Four Corners region. High temperatures for \nthis afternoon and Monday were manually raised by a few degrees, as \nobservations yesterday were slightly warmer than any of the NWP \nguidance; and this trend will hold as the subtropical ridge becomes \ncentered over the Deep South by Monday evening. Otherwise, dewpoints \nwere once again lowered to align closer to the drier MOS guidance; \nand pleasant fall weather will continue throughout the upcoming work \nweek across the entire CWA. A weakening backdoor cold front will \nalso sweep through the region during the day Tuesday and shift winds \ntowards the northeast post-FROPA. The passage of this cold front \nwill be rain-free; however, moistening of the boundary-layer will \noccur heading into Wednesday as the modified mP airmass trailing the \ncold front settles into the region. That said, high-level cirroform \nclouds will advect the region late Monday night, with the deck \nthickening heading into Wednesday as a remnant shortwave trough \nejects over the Corn Belt. Overnight lows by the middle part of the \nweek are forecast to be several degrees warmer compared to what has \nbeen observed recently, as the cirrus deck traps outgoing longwave \nradiation. Prospects for rain may return by late next weekend as the \nsubtropical ridge deamplifies and restores cyclonic flow aloft.",
